1
00:02:58,181 --> 00:03:04,181
After 700 years of doing what he was built for,
he'll discover what he was meant for.

2
00:05:56,363 --> 00:06:02,363
WALL·E is a animation, family, science fiction film directed by Andrew Stanton.

3
00:08:54,545 --> 00:09:00,545
What if mankind had to leave Earth and somebody
forgot to turn the last robot off?

4
00:11:52,727 --> 00:11:58,727
After hundreds of years doing what he was built for, WALL•E discovers a new
purpose in life when he meets a sleek search robot named EVE.

5
00:14:50,909 --> 00:14:56,909
EVE comes to realize that WALL•E has inadvertently stumbled upon the key to the
planet's future, and races back to space to report to the humans.

6
00:17:49,090 --> 00:17:55,090
Meanwhile, WALL•E chases EVE across the galaxy and sets into motion one of the
most imaginative adventures ever brought to the big screen.

7
00:20:47,272 --> 00:20:53,272
WALL·E / M-O (voice), played by Ben Burtt.

8
00:23:45,454 --> 00:23:51,454
EVE (voice), played by Elissa Knight.

9
00:26:43,636 --> 00:26:49,636
Captain (voice), played by Jeff Garlin.

10
00:29:41,818 --> 00:29:47,818
Shelby Forthright, BnL CEO, played by Fred Willard.

11
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:46,000
John (voice), played by John Ratzenberger.

12
00:35:38,181 --> 00:35:44,181
Mary (voice), played by Kathy Najimy.

13
00:38:36,363 --> 00:38:42,363
WALL-E (stylized with an interpunct as WALL·E) is a 2008 American animated romantic science fiction film directed by
Andrew Stanton, who co-wrote the screenplay with Jim Reardon, based on a story by Stanton and Pete Docter.

14
00:41:34,545 --> 00:41:40,545
Produced by Pixar Animation Studios for Walt Disney Pictures, the film stars the voices of Ben Burtt, Elissa Knight, Jeff
Garlin, John Ratzenberger, Kathy Najimy, and Sigourney Weaver, with Fred Willard in a live-action role.

15
00:44:32,727 --> 00:44:38,727
The film follows a solitary robot named WALL-E on a future,
uninhabitable, deserted Earth in 2805, left to clean up garbage.

16
00:47:30,909 --> 00:47:36,909
He is visited by a robot called EVE sent from the starship Axiom, with
whom he falls in love and pursues across the galaxy.

17
00:50:29,090 --> 00:50:35,090
After directing Finding Nemo, Stanton felt Pixar had created believable simulations of
underwater physics and was willing to direct a film set largely in space.

18
00:53:27,272 --> 00:53:33,272
WALL-E has minimal dialogue in its early sequences; many of the characters in the film do not have voices,
but instead communicate with body language and robotic sounds that were designed by Burtt.

19
00:56:25,454 --> 00:56:31,454
The film incorporates various topics including consumerism, corporatocracy, nostalgia, waste management, human
environmental impact and concerns, obesity/sedentary lifestyles, and global catastrophic risk.

20
00:59:23,636 --> 00:59:29,636
It is also Pixar's first animated film with segments
featuring live-action characters.

21
01:02:21,818 --> 01:02:27,818
Thomas Newman composed the film's musical score.

22
01:05:20,000 --> 01:05:26,000
The film cost $180 million to produce, a record-breaking
sum for an animated film at the time.

23
01:08:18,181 --> 01:08:24,181
Following Pixar tradition, WALL-E was paired with a short
film titled Presto for its theatrical release.

24
01:11:16,363 --> 01:11:22,363
WALL-E premiered at the Greek Theatre in Los Angeles on June 23,
2008, and was released in the United States on June 27.

25
01:14:14,545 --> 01:14:20,545
The film received critical acclaim for its animation, story, voice acting, characters, visuals,
score, sound design, screenplay, use of minimal dialogue, and scenes of romance.

26
01:17:12,727 --> 01:17:18,727
It was also commercially successful, grossing $521.3 million worldwide
and becoming the ninth-highest grossing film of 2008.

27
01:20:10,909 --> 01:20:16,909
It won the 2008 Golden Globe Award for Best Animated Feature Film, the 2009 Hugo Award for Best Long Form Dramatic Presentation, the final Nebula Award for
Best Script, the Saturn Award for Best Animated Film and the Academy Award for Best Animated Feature with five additional Oscar nominations.

28
01:23:09,090 --> 01:23:15,090
The film was widely named by critics and organizations, including the National Board of Review and American Film
Institute, as one of the best films of 2008, and is considered among the greatest animated films ever made.

29
01:26:07,272 --> 01:26:13,272
In 2021, WALL-E became the second Pixar feature film (after Toy Story), as well as the second animated film in the 21st century after Shrek, to be selected for
preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

30
01:29:05,454 --> 01:29:11,454
In September 2022, at the request of Stanton, Disney licensed WALL-E to The Criterion Collection, which re-released the film as a special
edition 4K Blu-Ray-standard Blu-ray combo pack on November 22, 2022, marking the first Pixar film to ever receive such an honor.

31
01:32:03,636 --> 01:32:09,636
The film explores themes of garbage, dystopia, space
travel, distant future, loneliness.

32
01:35:01,818 --> 01:35:07,818
The tone is thought_provoking.
